Exactly. It implies that our ignorance is probably asymmetric in both directions.

A dog can detect consequences of our capacities—machines move, doors open, food appears, humans coordinate strangely—without possessing the concepts of engineering, contracts, language, electricity, institutions, or mathematics that generated them.

We should assume the reciprocal possibility: other animals may inhabit capabilities whose effects we can observe while the operative experience remains unavailable to us.

Biology already gives obvious cases. A dog lives in an olfactory world with spatial and temporal structure we barely experience. A bat extracts geometry from returning sound. Some birds and other animals appear to use magnetic information. Sharks and rays detect electric fields. Many insects perceive polarization or wavelengths outside normal human vision. We can build instruments that tell us these channels exist, but instrumentation is not the same thing as knowing what it is like to have one integrated natively into cognition.

That suggests a deeper principle:

A creature’s apparent world is bounded not by reality, but by the interfaces evolution gave it for interrogating reality.

Jakob von Uexküll called something close to this an Umwelt: each organism lives in a perceptual-action world carved from the larger environment by its sensory and behavioral capacities.

And the really interesting part is not merely extra senses. A radically different sensor can produce a radically different kind of cognition.

If you continuously sensed magnetic orientation, your concepts of location might develop differently. If odor gave you a readable history of who passed through a place hours ago, memory and space might partly merge. If you could directly sense electric activity in nearby living bodies, “presence” might mean something very different.

So an animal’s advantage may be invisible to us in three different ways:

1. We lack the sensor.
2. We possess the sensor weakly but do not assign the information much cognitive importance.
3. We can measure the signal technologically but lack the native conceptual architecture built from a lifetime of experiencing it.

That third one is the big one.

It means there may be animal abilities we have already noticed but still fundamentally misunderstand because we’re translating them into human categories.

A useful inversion is:

A bat is not a deficient human with echolocation.
A human is a deficient bat with language.

Neither statement is globally true, of course. That’s the point. “Advantage” is indexed to an environment, objective, and interface.

And there is a strong analogy to what you were saying about people. With another human, you can observe behavior but not directly inhabit the private model generating it. Across species that delta becomes enormous:

same reality → different sensors → different representation → different salient features → different actions → different lived world.

So comparative cognition shouldn’t only ask, “How smart is this animal by human tests?”

It should also ask:

“What questions can this organism ask reality that we cannot?”

That is a much more dangerous—and much more interesting—question.